  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Fluoranthene and U373 MG, mediated through Ataxin-2. Fluoranthene is reported to have a target of Ataxin-2, which is itself a target of multiple compounds, including the complex molecule (1R,3R,4S,7S,9S,11S,13R,14R,15R)-4,11,13,14-tetrahydroxy-7-[(2Z,4R)-4-(hydroxymethyl)hex-2-en-2-yl]-3,9,15-trimethoxy-12,12-dimethyl-6,17-dioxabicyclo[11.3.1]heptadecan-5-one, and another compound, (2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-2-[(2R,3R,4R,6R)-6-[[(3S,8R,9S,10R,13R,14S,17S)-14-hydroxy-17-[(1S)-1-hydroxyethyl]-10,13-dimethyl-1,2,3,4,7,8,9,11,12,15,16,17-dodecahydrocyclopenta[a]phenanthren-3-yl]oxy]-4-methoxy-2-methyloxan-3-yl]oxy-4-methoxy-6-methyloxane-3,5-diol. These compounds all target U373 MG, indicating a potential pathway through which Fluoranthene may exert its effects on the cell line. The scores for the relationships are relatively high, suggesting a strong association between Fluoranthene and U373 MG through the Ataxin-2 pathway. The evidence is consistent across multiple paths, highlighting a possible mechanism involving Ataxin-2 and its downstream targets. However, the exact nature of Fluoranthene's interaction with Ataxin-2 and its subsequent effects on U373 MG remain to be fully elucidated.",
